  "source": {
    "short": "Álvarez, Compendio de la gramática de la lengua wayuu (2017)",
    "full": "José Álvarez, Compendio de la gramática de la lengua wayuu (2017) and Manual de la lengua wayuu. Spelling of the unified Wayuu alphabet."
  },
  "about": [
    "Wayuunaiki is spoken on the La Guajira Peninsula, in northern Colombia and northwestern Venezuela, and in cities such as Maracaibo and Riohacha. The Wayuu are one of the largest Indigenous peoples of both countries.",
    "It belongs to the Arawakan family, related to languages spoken from the Caribbean to the Amazon. Its name comes from wayuu «person, people» and anüiki «word, language».",
    "The Wayuu are organized in clans inherited through the mother, and their land is a desert of cacti and small settlements (rancherías). It is written with a 20-letter alphabet; long vowels are written double (aa) and ü is a vowel of its own."
  ],
